### protect: add vdc and convert to raid1
PLAN protect on /data (dry run, nothing was changed)
  profile: data raid1, metadata raid1, encrypted true
  WILL ERASE: /dev/vdc  6.0GiB  -  serial …-
checks:
  [ok  ] set-mounted: /data unprotected: one copy of the data only: a disk failure loses it
  [ok  ] no-missing-members: 0 missing; reconnect or replace first
  [ok  ] no-parity-raid: unprotected
  [ok  ] exists:/dev/vdc: whole disk from lsblk
  [ok  ] not-system:/dev/vdc: unused
  [ok  ] unused:/dev/vdc: no partitions or filesystem signatures
  [ok  ] writable:/dev/vdc: read_only=false
  [ok  ] healthy:/dev/vdc: unknown: /dev/vdc: Unable to detect device type
  [ok  ] big-enough:/dev/vdc: disk 6442450944 bytes, needs at least 1216348160 (allocated on the current member)
steps:
   1. sgdisk --zap-all /dev/vdc  # ERASES DATA
      # erase the partition tables on /dev/vdc
   2. sgdisk --new=1:0:0 --typecode=1:8309 --change-name=1:onemind-data /dev/vdc  # ERASES DATA
      # one partition over the whole disk, labelled onemind-data so it is found by label, not by sdX order
   3. udevadm settle
      # wait for the kernel to create /dev/vdc1
   4. cryptsetup luksFormat --type luks2 --batch-mode --key-file /root/lab.key /dev/vdc1  # ERASES DATA
      # LUKS2 encryption on the new member
   5. cryptsetup open --key-file /root/lab.key /dev/vdc1 onemind-data-vdc
      # unlock it as /dev/mapper/onemind-data-vdc
   6. btrfs device add /dev/mapper/onemind-data-vdc /data
      # join the new member to the set
   7. btrfs balance start -dconvert=raid1,soft -mconvert=raid1,soft /data
      # write a second copy of every chunk on the other disk (soft: skips chunks already converted, so a restart after a power cut resumes)
   8. btrfs filesystem df /data
      # verify: Data, Metadata and System show the new profile
plan_sha256: a46a43772d7cf6319f8976d9af387f74c694177d26b2e98b77749726aef676aa

